n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{300}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}$ must be *positive* because the cosine ratio is *always* positive in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058596 :answer-field/correct "answer-1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8502863-a146-578b-9ce5-4158af8863a9" "answer-9f9685b2-1cd2-5b88-a29e-364e31bf2620" "answer-817c85f0-9a1d-5b8c-8485-5cfad53e03c4" "answer-1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" "answer-4131b12a-0fcf-57ab-9612-46354b4fd9d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8502863-a146-578b-9ce5-4158af8863a9" :answer/id #uuid "d8502863-a146-578b-9ce5-4158af8863a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9685b2-1cd2-5b88-a29e-364e31bf2620" :answer/id #uuid "9f9685b2-1cd2-5b88-a29e-364e31bf2620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-817c85f0-9a1d-5b8c-8485-5cfad53e03c4" :answer/id #uuid "817c85f0-9a1d-5b8c-8485-5cfad53e03c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" :answer/id #uuid "1baf1391-fef5-5ca5-81f7-b718e33d0414" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4131b12a-0fcf-57ab-9612-46354b4fd9d7" :answer/id #uuid "4131b12a-0fcf-57ab-9612-46354b4fd9d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0f9ed04-9025-5f06-a930-77e84f985c93" :question/id #uuid "b0f9ed04-9025-5f06-a930-77e84f985c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-156598" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${280}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/2a/2ad04709282b00c6a185891c219017cbfb55af57845a7cfa28d1f1cc4b677c58.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={280}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{280}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ must be *positive* because the cosine ratio is *always* positive in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ad9542b-ebee-506e-86a5-ac791d978f7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ad9542b-ebee-506e-86a5-ac791d978f7a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad9542b-ebee-506e-86a5-ac791d978f7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb3e4c0d-7d43-5e0a-8d3a-22094be279de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-590fcb10-5fff-513d-ba1f-8b1ac85823c1" "answer-2013e8aa-4f6c-5cf8-86d1-fb6021658fd9" "answer-e7ad56e7-b175-54e7-bc99-0065661c5f76" "answer-bb3e4c0d-7d43-5e0a-8d3a-22094be279de" "answer-cac13f42-f2fe-540f-85c0-4dd91d075200"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-590fcb10-5fff-513d-ba1f-8b1ac85823c1" :answer/id #uuid "590fcb10-5fff-513d-ba1f-8b1ac85823c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2013e8aa-4f6c-5cf8-86d1-fb6021658fd9" :answer/id #uuid "2013e8aa-4f6c-5cf8-86d1-fb6021658fd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{10}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ad56e7-b175-54e7-bc99-0065661c5f76" :answer/id #uuid "e7ad56e7-b175-54e7-bc99-0065661c5f76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{10}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb3e4c0d-7d43-5e0a-8d3a-22094be279de" :answer/id #uuid "bb3e4c0d-7d43-5e0a-8d3a-22094be279de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cac13f42-f2fe-540f-85c0-4dd91d075200" :answer/id #uuid "cac13f42-f2fe-540f-85c0-4dd91d075200" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ace8cb21-d5d1-5afe-a9db-0af7bfa7a631" :question/id #uuid "ace8cb21-d5d1-5afe-a9db-0af7bfa7a631" :question/math-academy-id "q-156608" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${173}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/bb/bb5ea51c0fabeb994c161a53ed5bdc0869c988eb8ab60993e7e1fe96a058040f.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={173}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{173}^{∘} \\\\  & ={7}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27ad1e7a-41f5-5f06-bfec-2551e828337a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27ad1e7a-41f5-5f06-bfec-2551e828337a" :answer-field/id #uuid "27ad1e7a-41f5-5f06-bfec-2551e828337a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-95e44251-8193-5969-9031-ac6f0fd94059" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09205301-c82f-54fc-9318-8476966aa8e2" "answer-95e44251-8193-5969-9031-ac6f0fd94059" "answer-c5893060-b54e-590e-a685-f88a2c9439e4" "answer-4181b1d2-2fb7-5749-bf3d-257c6cfe7b33" "answer-419395b4-36cd-5811-9644-53510deded67"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09205301-c82f-54fc-9318-8476966aa8e2" :answer/id #uuid "09205301-c82f-54fc-9318-8476966aa8e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{187}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e44251-8193-5969-9031-ac6f0fd94059" :answer/id #uuid "95e44251-8193-5969-9031-ac6f0fd94059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5893060-b54e-590e-a685-f88a2c9439e4" :answer/id #uuid "c5893060-b54e-590e-a685-f88a2c9439e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{97}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4181b1d2-2fb7-5749-bf3d-257c6cfe7b33" :answer/id #uuid "4181b1d2-2fb7-5749-bf3d-257c6cfe7b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419395b4-36cd-5811-9644-53510deded67" :answer/id #uuid "419395b4-36cd-5811-9644-53510deded67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{97}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e92a8145-93d8-5d18-8c79-868664e2dfd3" :question/id #uuid "e92a8145-93d8-5d18-8c79-868664e2dfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-156602" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${280}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/70/70c2ef3ed7982e487a23eb8332a3740d65800a9b3bac73b8209b2c3cdb9fa210.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={280}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{280}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f65401a-e6e6-5d4c-ba14-deb04c599d5a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f65401a-e6e6-5d4c-ba14-deb04c599d5a" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f65401a-e6e6-5d4c-ba14-deb04c599d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7df0b495-6e8c-559e-95ec-72a27817acf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-438bfe93-1158-561a-864f-7d811e0ddb1a" "answer-5cc95e5c-e99d-520d-b942-a87b9c547366" "answer-7df0b495-6e8c-559e-95ec-72a27817acf9" "answer-27a16869-0635-54e7-9a6f-d0e4ed1f11f1" "answer-6f957ff1-aaff-5a69-80d2-02deed974011"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-438bfe93-1158-561a-864f-7d811e0ddb1a" :answer/id #uuid "438bfe93-1158-561a-864f-7d811e0ddb1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{100}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cc95e5c-e99d-520d-b942-a87b9c547366" :answer/id #uuid "5cc95e5c-e99d-520d-b942-a87b9c547366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{10}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7df0b495-6e8c-559e-95ec-72a27817acf9" :answer/id #uuid "7df0b495-6e8c-559e-95ec-72a27817acf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27a16869-0635-54e7-9a6f-d0e4ed1f11f1" :answer/id #uuid "27a16869-0635-54e7-9a6f-d0e4ed1f11f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{100}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f957ff1-aaff-5a69-80d2-02deed974011" :answer/id #uuid "6f957ff1-aaff-5a69-80d2-02deed974011" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060971 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the cosine and sine ratios for *acute* angles can be defined in the following ways:\n\n- Using a right triangle. If $\\theta$ is an acute angle in a right triangle, then\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}.\n$$\n\n- Using the unit circle. If $\\theta$ is a central angle made by a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle in the first quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y.\n$$\n\nWe can't define trigonometric ratios for non-acute angles using right triangles because a right triangle can't have angles larger than $90^\\circ.$ However, we can *extend* these ratios for non-acute angles using unit circles.\n\nTo do that, let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in any quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/61/612d468ea23fefb6bbb33bd676a1930604b283475f36be08541419776bb580bd.png)\n\nWe *define* the sine and cosine of $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- the cosine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $x$-coordinate of $P,$ and\n\n- the sine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $y$-coordinate of $P.$\n\nThis way, our trigonometric ratios make sense for any angle, even if this angle is larger than $90^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060861 :tutorial/content "We can use reference angles to evaluate trigonometric ratios for angles outside the first quadrant. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the value of $\\cos (135^\\circ),$ corresponding to the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the unit circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/bef50a070785e4bb86867ba6404942fd7eaa04b60720adfb04176bafe2fa2121.png)\n\nThe reference angle for $\\theta=135^\\circ$ is $\\theta_R=45^\\circ.$ Let's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33b208e44aaa556a67ebaf7a6c1117b09adf863c56deb1bc1f5590e289062c4b.png)\n\nLet $P'$ be the image of $P$ under a reflection across the $y$-axis. This gives a pair of congruent angles of measure $45^\\circ,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/c4/c4875682498edaadc74a8af238fdf3293b4820025256df78e60867940061785e.png)\n\nAccording to the definition, $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ is the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$ But this is the same as the $x$-coordinate of the point $P'$ *taken with the opposite sign.* \n\nThe $x$-coordinate of $P'$ equals the cosine of the reference angle. \n\n$$\n\\cos(45^\\circ)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\, \\underbrace{135^\\circ}_{\\theta} \\,) = - \\cos(\\, \\underbrace{45^\\circ}_{\\theta_R} \\,) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we simplified the evaluation of $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ by evaluating the cosine of the corresponding reference angle $\\theta_R=45^\\circ$ *with an additional sign adjustment* for the final result. \n\nWe can use a similar argument to show that, since $y = \\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(135^\\circ) = \\sin(45^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we do **NOT** change the sign because the $y$-coordinates of $P'$ and $P$ *are equal.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060972 :tutorial/content "We can calculate a trigonometric ratio of *any* angle $\\theta$ by finding the ratio of the corresponding reference angle and adjusting the sign depending on which quadrant our angle lies in.\n\nTo do this, we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Find the reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the ratio for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting ratio is positive or negative.\n\nWe must do the last step because the ratio for $\\theta_R$ will always be nonnegative, but the ratio for $\\theta$ may not be.\n\nBut how do we determine whether the ratio for $\\theta$ is positive or negative? This depends on the trigonometric ratio and which quadrant the angle is in.\n\nTo help us remember when various trigonometric ratios are positive, we use the mnemonic \"**A**ll **S**tudents **T**ake **C**alculus.\" \n\n- **A**ll of the trigonometric ratios are positive in quadrant I\n\n- **S**ine (and only sine) is positive in quadrant II\n\n- **T**angent (and only tangent) is positive in quadrant III\n\n- **C**osine (and only cosine) is positive in quadrant IV\n\nWe can represent this in a so-called CAST diagram, shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a6d43985058f1afd33272537def49886177aceab115f0084b1cf9d08ba78c43.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider $\\sin 140^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/da/da7ca1a89b91d157ad97a3800d8c2d67efb467a8ceaa8f3c964bea5275ecfc5d.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = 140^\\circ$ is the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin{140^\\circ},$ and therefore we're interested in $\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin{40^\\circ}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin{140^\\circ}$ must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin{40^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059904 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
